    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c32d7250_299a9022",
        "filename": "zephyr/drivers/cros_flash/cros_flash_em32f967.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 170,
      "author": {
        "id": 1177449
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-08T07:27:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Good point.\n\nOn EM32F967 the flash write protection is configured using two Protect Area\nregisters. Each Protect Area describes a single **contiguous** protection\nregion using (start address, size, enable), and the hardware does not allow\nexpressing a non‑contiguous pattern within a single protection region.\n\nIt is possible to configure two protection regions with a gap between them\n(e.g. protect area 1 covers one range, protect area 2 covers a later range),\nwhich is an intentional hardware design and is handled by the driver’s policy\nwhen mapping protection ranges to EC semantics.\n\nHowever, the hardware cannot generate a pattern where a single protection\nregion itself is fragmented (e.g. protected → unprotected → protected again\nwithin one region). Given these constraints, the driver does not expect\narbitrary non‑contiguous bank patterns, and additional inconsistency checks\nfor such cases are not required here.",
      "parentUuid": "5096e439_9f560bbd",
      "revId": "abc5fe520eeb4fa78aa11da4a1dd09581b478c6f",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "76c0da91_59c8611e",
        "filename": "zephyr/drivers/cros_flash/cros_flash_em32f967.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 170,
      "author": {
        "id": 1438457
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-08T13:53:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So IIUC, the \"fragmented\" protection ranges are possible in theory, right? Like no protection -\u003e range 1 -\u003e no protection -\u003e range 2. Idea of this function is to check the current protection in hardware. I understand that the cros-flash driver doesn\u0027t expect/configure such state but I\u0027m talking about cases like a bug in code, other code changes protection ranges for any reason, uninitialized protection state, etc. So I\u0027m thinking it would be nice to check if everything is good with the hardware protection (expected state) if that\u0027s possible.",
      "parentUuid": "c32d7250_299a9022",
      "revId": "abc5fe520eeb4fa78aa11da4a1dd09581b478c6f",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
